  "implementation": [
    "DataMap now contains one private typed owner of actual BaseMapStorage plus actual DataMapNode<K,V> chains. The previous Vec<Vec<AllocatedBox<Entry>>> and duplicate count/modulus/allocator metadata are gone. A transient rehash destination vector is staging only, not authoritative ownership or topology.",
    "Insertion allocates a real node before constructing its inherited value and then key; a successful node is prepended and the base count incremented. Existing borrowed construction and assignment order tests run against this actual storage.",
    "Resize allocates through native BaseMapResize, hashes old buckets in native chain order and prepends nodes into the replacement buckets without moving keys or values. Only MapLinkNode prefixes are mutably reborrowed during link rewrites, preserving retained item pointer provenance.",
    "Removal decrements the base count and unlinks before key/value destruction. Clear(allocator) drains under the old allocator and only then installs the requested resource, preserving the source operation order.",
    "Legacy cursors and standard ranges traverse the same real nodes. Mutable standard ranges advance before lending each distinct value, and legacy mutable postfix snapshots borrow the cursor to prevent competing mutable references.",
    "Private typed storage has narrowly bounded unsafe Send(K,V:Send) and Sync(K,V:Sync): it solely owns the entire graph, mutable access requires its exclusive borrow, and the allocator is thread-safe. Public erased BaseMapStorage and raw nodes remain unchanged and do not gain blanket thread traits.",
    "The former K/V RefUnwindSafe requirements on owner UnwindSafe and RefUnwindSafe are preserved; H retains its independent native Rust trait bounds. Heap values and ranges remain Unpin even for PhantomPinned values. Positive/negative trait assertions and scoped-thread tests validate these bounds.",
    "Shared destructor panic capture was extracted from the existing SparseArray implementation. Both typed owners now reuse it; SparseArray original ten tests and all selected safety/cursor/raw-engine regressions pass normally and under Miri.",
    "Map<K,H> continues to delegate behavior to DataMap<K,(),H>; its original twenty tests, borrowed-copy tests, key replacement/algebra/cursor contracts and retained key pointer regression pass on the actual new chains."
  ],
  "safety_extensions": [
    "A Rust hash panic during rehash drops the pending bucket allocation without changing old links. Ordinary valid native hash callback order is preserved, but the Rust panic guarantee is stronger than native throwing-hasher behavior.",
    "Rust destructor panics are outside native noexcept deletion behavior. Each node release uses the shared typed deallocation guard, Clear drains remaining nodes and restores canonical count/table state before resuming the first panic. SparseArray regressions also cover panicking secondary payload destruction.",
    "Rust Clone::clone_from remains build-replacement-then-destroy semantics, copying source allocator/hasher/modulus. Native Assign is a separate method preserving destination allocator/hasher/table and is tested independently. Failed clone_from keeps the old destination valid.",
    "Over-aligned Rust values, zero-sized mapped values, stable pointers after other-node removals and multiple simultaneous mutable iterator values are checked by Miri. Finite fixtures do not claim closure over all C++ user-defined move constructors or variadic packs."
